WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:10.830
Malcolm Grant: May we move to item ten on
the agenda, which is the conflicts of interest

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.349
paper? Ian, over to you.
Ian Dodge: Thank you, I will be brief. At

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:21.180
its last meeting, the Board approved for consultation
strengthened arrangements for how CCGs manage

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:26.710
conflicts of interest. The core element, as
set out in paragraph four of the paper, has

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:33.390
been broadly welcomed by those responding.
In response to the feedback we have received,

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:39.249
we propose to make a number of specific adjustments
as set out in paragraph seven. So subject

00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:44.909
to Board agreement, and delegation of the
approval of the final version of the statutory

00:00:44.909 --> 00:00:51.879
guidance to myself and to Paul, the new requirements
will commence in June.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:07.940
Bruce Keogh: Ian only 38 CCGs have responded.
Is that because the remaining CCGs are completely

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.570
happy with the guidance? It seems to me that
since this applies to them, that is quite

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.009
a small sample size.
Ian Dodge: I would say the reason why we only

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:22.770
had 38 respondents is because of the depth
of the engagements that occurred with CCGs

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.930
prior to the consultation.
Bruce Keogh: Okay, yes.

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:31.869
Malcolm Grant: Can I just say, I think this
is a very good piece of work. I think we should

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:38.009
commend the way in which the policies have
developed, the largely almost all positive

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:46.719
responses we have had from CCGs, and it forms
a very important basis for the broader pan‑healthcare

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:53.140
group that I am chairing. So we will no doubt
pick up learning from this exercise which

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:59.579
can be transmitted into the broader view.
Are there any further questions for Ian? No,

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:05.119
in that case, Ian, thank you very much.
I would like to turn now to item 11, the diabetes

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.360
prevention programme.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:17.220
Karen Wheeler: I just wanted to share our
progress on this important agenda item for

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:25.090
NHS England during the past year. Obviously,
this is an important programme for the Five

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:31.000
Year Forward View, as part of the prevention
programme, and demonstrates a good partnership

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.890
between ourselves and Public Health England
and Diabetes UK in working on how we can try

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:45.849
to tackle prevention of diabetes more systematically.
We have been through the procurement process

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:54.409
to procure some providers to provide services
nationally for patients, and those have been

00:02:54.409 --> 00:03:00.299
procured and are in the phase of mobilising
as we speak now. In parallel, we have been

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:09.159
looking to identify people who are appropriate
to be referred to those programmes, and 7,000

00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:17.739
people are in the process of being referred
by the end of March. We hope that by July,

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:24.549
we will be at 10,000 people, and that is when
the services will be largely mobilised for

00:03:24.549 --> 00:03:32.189
our initial pilot areas, our initial set of
ten areas who have been awarded local contracts.

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:42.450
That will then extend to 2017, in June, and
overall we hope to achieve 40,000 people being

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:55.390
referred during 2016/2017, and by 2019/2020,
we should be getting up to 190,000 per annum

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:59.430
for the programme, with all the various initiatives
that are going on.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:04.340
It was to ensure you that we are making real
progress and have started to deliver some

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:12.969
real changes to people on the ground. It is
worth reminding people that this is in pilot

00:04:12.969 --> 00:04:18.180
mode; we have only procured these services
for a relatively short period of time so that

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:28.900
we can evaluate them before we go for a more
substantially longer term, and embedded national

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:35.070
service across the country. We will be doing
a lot of evaluation about the approach for

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:41.110
this programme as we go forward.
I am very happy to take questions.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:51.920
Michelle Mitchell: What opportunities do you
think are there for new digital service interventions

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:59.460
and the potential partnerships with the voluntary
sector in scaling up at reduced cost, and

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.150
reaching more people?
Karen Wheeler: That is a really good question,

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:10.840
thank you. There is clearly a huge opportunity
and we are exploring that very proactively.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:17.180
We have built into the contracts in any case
that we wanted our existing providers to pay

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:23.389
attention to innovation and digital usage
as well as face-to-face, but these are largely

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:28.270
face-to-face at this stage. We are working
very closely with Diabetes UK, of course,

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:35.190
who have a very strong digital presence in
their risk assessment tool, which they offer,

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:41.210
and we are looking to see with them if there
are opportunities for us to use that and expand

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:48.789
that reach into a wider group of people. In
parallel with that, we are working through

00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:55.569
the programme on what might be an additional
offer that we would be able to procure and

00:05:55.569 --> 00:06:06.460
expand nationally, and that could well be
a way of very significantly increasing scale

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:14.210
across the country very rapidly, by getting
to people more directly and more quickly.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:21.840
Obviously, we will need to test as we do that
how effective that is, but if we can get the

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:28.289
scale, I think it is hugely valuable, and
it is definitely something we will progress.

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:32.919
Michelle Mitchell: What sort of timelines
are you looking at for concluding your thinking

00:06:32.919 --> 00:06:37.990
and testing in the digital space?
Karen Wheeler: We have already had an information

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:46.520
day with suppliers during May, specifically
in order to get as much visibility of this

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:52.819
with suppliers, so we can start the conversation,
as we are now, with suppliers about what sorts

00:06:52.819 --> 00:06:59.410
of things we could be doing. That has already
happened, and we got a much larger number

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:06.240
than we expected, which was fantastic: large
and small scale, and different sectors, as

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:14.039
well as digital specialists. We are hoping
that, during the course of this calendar year,

00:07:14.039 --> 00:07:20.860
we will have been able to specify procurement
and will have started to launch procurement,

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:26.879
but we are trying to still navigate exactly
what the best route is for doing that, so

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:33.910
that we do not overspecify the work with suppliers
who are able to help us think about what the

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:39.229
best possible approach would be, and we will
be doing that with Diabetes UK, as well, because

00:07:39.229 --> 00:07:45.569
of their expertise.
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Karen.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.900
I ask the Board to receive the update.

